IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cla-dates`
IMPORT `cla-core`

-- Civil Liability Act 1936 (SA) -- THE CLAIMANT'S SEAT: the person injured in an accident, or the family member (or
-- personal representative) claiming for a death. Every question is asked as that person would ask it, from facts that
-- person can state; the court's judgments are asked as conditions. The rules are in cla-core.l4; this module restates
-- none of them.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website, as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025 (unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.

§ `The claimant's questions`

§§ `A claim for a death (Part 5)`
-- Situation: a member of your family has died because of someone else's wrongful act, neglect or default.

@export Is the action for my family member's wrongful death in South Australia brought for my benefit (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 24(1), (5))?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse (legally married on the day the cause of action arose), domestic partner (declared under the Family Relationships Act 1975, or in a registered relationship, on that day), father or mother, grandparent or step-parent, brother or sister (half- and step- count), child, grandchild or step-child, or none of these
      `I renounced it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have renounced the benefit of the action by written notice served on the executor or administrator
GIVETH A `Death action benefit answer`
`Is the death action brought for my benefit` MEANS
    `is the death action for my benefit (s 24(1), (5))` `my relationship` `I renounced it`

@export May I bring the action for my family member's wrongful death myself, rather than the executor or administrator (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 24(1), 25, 27(1))?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse, domestic partner, father or mother, grandparent or step-parent, brother or sister (half- and step- count), child, grandchild or step-child, or none of these
      `there is an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has an executor (under a will) or an administrator (appointed by the court)
      `an action has been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action for the death under Part 5 has already been commenced by anyone
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day I would commence` IS A DATE @desc The day you would file the action
GIVETH A `Who may bring the death action answer`
`May I bring the death action myself` MEANS
    `who may bring the Part 5 action (ss 24, 25, 27)` `my relationship` `there is an executor or administrator` `an action has been commenced` `the day of death` `the day I would commence`

@export By when must the action for my family member's wrongful death be commenced (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 25: within three years after the death)?
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the death action be commenced` MEANS
    `the last day to commence the Part 5 action (s 25)` `the day of death`

@export Was (or is) the action for my family member's wrongful death commenced within the three years after the death (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 25)?
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `it has been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action has been commenced (filed in court)
      `the day it was commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day it was commenced (any day if it has not been)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Time limit answer`
`Was the death action commenced in time` MEANS
    `was the Part 5 action commenced in time (s 25)` `the day of death` `it has been commenced` `the day it was commenced` `today`

@export Is this payment we received because of the death taken into account (deducted) when the damages for the death are assessed (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 24(2aa))?
GIVEN `the payment` IS A `Payment on the death` @desc The kind of payment: life assurance or insurance; a gratuity paid because of the death; superannuation; a contributory medical, hospital, death or funeral benefit scheme; a Commonwealth, United Kingdom or South Australian social service benefit or pension; dust disease damages recovered for the estate; or another payment
GIVETH A `Deduction answer`
`Is this payment deducted from the damages for the death` MEANS
    `is this payment taken into account (s 24(2aa))` `the payment`

@export Can the damages for my family member's death include the medical expenses of the injury and the funeral expenses (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 24(2a))?
GIVEN `we paid them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the expenses were incurred by a family member for whose benefit the action is brought (spouse, domestic partner, parent, brother, sister or child)
GIVETH A `Expenses answer`
`Can the death damages include medical and funeral expenses` MEANS
    `can the damages include medical and funeral expenses (s 24(2a))` `we paid them`

@export What solatium (damages for grief) can I get for the wrongful death of my child (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 28, 30)?
GIVEN `I am the father or mother` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the child's father or mother (grandparents and step-parents do not count here)
      `the child was an infant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was an infant (under 18) when the child died
      `the death was wrongful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default that would have let the child sue had the child lived
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the child died
      `the court refuses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court refuses solatium because of your conduct towards, or relations with, the child, or another sufficient reason (usually no)
GIVETH A `Solatium answer`
`What solatium can I get for my child's death` MEANS
    `solatium for the death of a child (s 28)` `I am the father or mother` `the child was an infant` `the death was wrongful` `the day of death` `the court refuses`

@export What solatium (damages for grief) can I get for the wrongful death of my spouse or domestic partner (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 29, 30)?
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died (only the spouse or the domestic partner can claim this)
      `the death was wrongful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default that would have let them sue had they lived
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day they died
      `the court refuses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court refuses solatium because of your conduct towards, or relations with, them, or another sufficient reason (usually no)
GIVETH A `Solatium answer`
`What solatium can I get for my partner's death` MEANS
    `solatium for the death of a spouse or domestic partner (s 29)` `my relationship` `the death was wrongful` `the day of death` `the court refuses`

§§ `Defences about risk and criminal conduct (Part 6)`

@export Am I taken to have known about an obvious risk, so that the defence of voluntary assumption of risk can be used against me (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 37)?
GIVEN `the defendant raises it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing raises the defence of voluntary assumption of risk (volenti non fit injuria)
      `the risk was obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the risk obvious to a reasonable person in your position (patent, or a matter of common knowledge, even if unlikely)
      `a reasonable person would have avoided it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds a reasonable person in your position would have taken steps, which you did not take, to avoid the risk
      `I prove I was not actually aware` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you prove, on the balance of probabilities, that you were not actually aware of the type or kind of risk
GIVETH A `Awareness answer`
`Am I taken to have known of the obvious risk` MEANS
    `voluntary assumption of an obvious risk (s 37)` `the defendant raises it` `the risk was obvious` `a reasonable person would have avoided it` `I prove I was not actually aware`

@export Did the person I am suing owe me a duty to warn me of the risk that caused my harm, if it was obvious (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 38)?
GIVEN `the risk was obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the risk obvious to a reasonable person in your position
      `I asked about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked them for advice or information about the risk
      `a law required the warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a written law required them to warn you of the risk
      `a health care risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a risk of death or personal injury to you from a health care service they provided
GIVETH A `Duty to warn answer`
`Did they have to warn me of the obvious risk` MEANS
    `duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 38)` `the risk was obvious` `I asked about the risk` `a law required the warning` `a health care risk`

@export Can I sue in negligence for harm from a risk that could not have been avoided by reasonable care (an inherent risk) (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 39)?
GIVEN `unavoidable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the risk could not have been avoided by the exercise of reasonable care and skill
      `my claim is for a failure to warn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is that they failed to warn you of the risk
GIVETH A `Inherent risk answer`
`Can I sue for an inherent risk` MEANS
    `inherent risk (s 39)` `unavoidable` `my claim is for a failure to warn`

@export Is my personal injury claim excluded because I was committing a crime when the accident happened (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 43)?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident (a vehicle under the Motor Vehicles Act 1959); in another motor accident (a train or tram, for example); in an accident caused wholly or partly by negligence, another person's unintentional tort or a breach of a contractual duty of care; or in some other way
      `the finding` IS A `Offence finding` @desc Whether you were convicted or acquitted of an indictable offence you were said to be committing at the time, or, if neither, whether the court is satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that you were committing one
      `my conduct contributed materially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that your conduct contributed materially to the risk of injury
      `exceptional and harsh` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that your case is exceptional and the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly in it
GIVETH A `Criminal conduct answer`
`Is my claim excluded for criminal conduct` MEANS
    `exclusion for criminal conduct (s 43)` `how it arose` `the finding` `my conduct contributed materially` `exceptional and harsh`

§§ `Contributory negligence (Part 7)`
-- Situation: the person you are suing says you were partly to blame.

@export I had been drinking or taking drugs when I was injured: are my damages presumed to be reduced, and by at least how much (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 46, 48)?
GIVEN `my blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc Your blood alcohol concentration at or about the time of the accident as found by the court, in grams per 100 millilitres of blood (for example 0.08); 0 if none was found
      `I was otherwise intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were otherwise under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that your capacity to exercise due care and skill was impaired
      `contributory negligence is alleged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing alleges you were contributorily negligent
      `it did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that your intoxication did not contribute to the accident
      `not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove your intoxication was not self-induced
      `prescribed drugs as instructed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove your intoxication was wholly from drugs taken as a medical practitioner prescribed or instructed, while following the practitioner's and the manufacturer's instructions
      `I was the driver in a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor accident and you were the driver of a vehicle involved in it
      `I could not control the vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence shows you were so much under the influence as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
GIVETH A `Intoxication answer`
`Are my damages reduced for my intoxication` MEANS
    `your own intoxication (s 46)` `my blood alcohol` `I was otherwise intoxicated` `contributory negligence is alleged` `it did not contribute` `not self-induced` `prescribed drugs as instructed` `I was the driver in a motor accident` `I could not control the vehicle`

@export I relied on (or was a passenger with) someone who was intoxicated: are my damages reduced, and by how much (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 47, 48)?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in years at the time of the accident
      `I relied on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you relied on the care and skill of the intoxicated person
      `I was their passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a passenger in a motor vehicle they were driving
      `their blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc Their blood alcohol concentration as found by the court, in grams per 100 millilitres (0 if none was found)
      `they were otherwise intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were otherwise under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that their capacity to exercise due care and skill was impaired
      `I knew or ought to have known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were aware, or ought to have been aware, that they were intoxicated
      `their negligence caused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident was caused through their negligence
      `contributory negligence is alleged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing alleges you were contributorily negligent
      `it did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove their intoxication did not contribute to the accident
      `I could not have avoided the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk
      `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor accident
      `they could not control the vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence shows the driver was so much under the influence as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
GIVETH A `Fixed reduction answer`
`Are my damages reduced for relying on an intoxicated person` MEANS
    `relying on an intoxicated person (s 47)` `my age` `I relied on them` `I was their passenger` `their blood alcohol` `they were otherwise intoxicated` `I knew or ought to have known` `their negligence caused it` `contributory negligence is alleged` `it did not contribute` `I could not have avoided the risk` `a motor accident` `they could not control the vehicle`

@export I was not wearing a seatbelt or helmet, or rode outside the passenger compartment: are my damages reduced (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 49)?
GIVEN `a motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were injured in a motor accident
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in years at the time of the accident
      `no seatbelt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a seatbelt as the Road Traffic Act 1961 required
      `no helmet, and it mattered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a safety helmet as the Road Traffic Act 1961 required, and that contributed to the accident or the extent of your injury
      `outside the compartment, and it mattered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a passenger outside the vehicle's passenger compartment, and that contributed to the accident or the extent of your injury
      `I could not have avoided the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk (counts only for riding outside the passenger compartment)
GIVETH A `Fixed reduction answer`
`Are my damages reduced for no seatbelt or helmet` MEANS
    `seatbelt, helmet or passenger compartment (s 49)` `a motor accident` `my age` `no seatbelt` `no helmet, and it mattered` `outside the compartment, and it mattered` `I could not have avoided the risk`

@export After the reductions for contributory negligence, how much of my damages is left (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 50)?
GIVEN `my full damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages assessed as if there were no contributory negligence, in dollars
      `the court's percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage reduction the court fixes for your ordinary contributory negligence, including any intoxication (0 if none)
      `the intoxication minimum` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum percentage for your own intoxication, from the s 46 question (25 or 50; 0 if no presumption)
      `the reduction for relying on an intoxicated person` IS A NUMBER @desc The fixed percentage from the s 47 question (25 or 50; 0 if none)
      `the reduction for seatbelt or helmet` IS A NUMBER @desc The fixed percentage from the s 49 question (25; 0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is left after contributory negligence` MEANS
    `the damages after contributory negligence (s 50)` `my full damages` `the court's percentage` `the intoxication minimum` `the reduction for relying on an intoxicated person` `the reduction for seatbelt or helmet`

§§ `Damages for personal injury (Part 8)`

@export Can I get damages for pain and suffering (non-economic loss) for my injury, and how much (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 52; Civil Liability Regulations 2013 reg 7)?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident (a vehicle under the Motor Vehicles Act 1959); in another motor accident (a train or tram, for example); in an accident caused wholly or partly by negligence, another person's unintentional tort or a breach of a contractual duty of care; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `impaired for at least 7 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your ability to lead a normal life was significantly impaired by the injury for at least 7 days
      `my medical expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc The medical expenses reasonably incurred in connection with the injury, in dollars (fees of doctors and therapists, hospital, medicines and appliances)
      `the scale value` IS A NUMBER @desc The scale value the court assigns to your non-economic loss: 0 to 60 in general, or the injury scale value 0 to 100 after an MVA motor accident on or after 1 July 2013
      `exceptional and harsh` IS A BOOLEAN @desc After an MVA motor accident with an injury scale value of 10 or less: whether the court is satisfied the consequences are exceptional compared with the same injury and the threshold would be harsh and unjust
      `CPI Sep before` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the accident
      `CPI Sep 2001` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2001, on the same base
      `CPI Sep 2002` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2002, on the same base
      `CPI Mar adjustment` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the March quarter of the year of the last 1 July on or before the accident (only used for an MVA motor accident on or after 1 July 2014)
      `CPI Mar 2013` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the March quarter 2013, on the same base
GIVETH A `Non-economic loss answer`
`Can I get damages for non-economic loss` MEANS
    `damages for non-economic loss (s 52)` `how it arose` `the accident day` `impaired for at least 7 days` `my medical expenses` `the scale value` `exceptional and harsh` `CPI Sep before` `CPI Sep 2001` `CPI Sep 2002` `CPI Mar adjustment` `CPI Mar 2013`

@export What is the prescribed minimum of medical expenses that lets me claim non-economic loss for an accident in my year (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 3(1), 52(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `CPI Sep before` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the accident
      `CPI Sep 2001` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2001, on the same base
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the prescribed minimum for my accident` MEANS
    `the prescribed sum` 2750 `for` `the accident day` `with` `CPI Sep before` `and` `CPI Sep 2001`

@export Can I get damages for mental harm (psychological injury) from the accident (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 53)?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident; in another motor accident; in an accident caused wholly or partly by negligence, another person's unintentional tort or a breach of a contractual duty of care; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `injured or at the scene` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were physically injured in the accident, or present at the scene when it happened
      `close family of a victim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a parent, spouse, domestic partner or child of a person killed, injured or endangered in the accident
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of mental harm` @desc Pure mental harm (not a consequence of a bodily injury to you), or consequential mental harm (a consequence of your bodily injury)
      `a recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mental harm is a recognised psychiatric illness
GIVETH A `Mental harm answer`
`Can I get damages for mental harm` MEANS
    `damages for mental harm (s 53)` `how it arose` `the accident day` `injured or at the scene` `close family of a victim` `the kind` `a recognised psychiatric illness`

@export What is the most I can be awarded in total for loss of earning capacity (the prescribed maximum) (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 3(1), 54(2))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `CPI Sep before` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the accident
      `CPI Sep 2001` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2001, on the same base
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most for loss of earning capacity` MEANS
    `the prescribed maximum for` `the accident day` `with` `CPI Sep before` `and` `CPI Sep 2001`

@export After a road accident, can I get damages for loss of future earning capacity, given my injury scale value (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 56A(2), (3))?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident; in another motor accident; in an accident caused by negligence or similar; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `the scale value` IS A NUMBER @desc The injury scale value (0 to 100) that applies to your injury under the regulations
      `exceptional and harsh` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the consequences for your future earning capacity are exceptional and the threshold would be harsh and unjust
GIVETH A `Future earning capacity answer`
`Can I get damages for loss of future earning capacity` MEANS
    `the threshold for future earning capacity (s 56A(2), (3))` `how it arose` `the accident day` `the scale value` `exceptional and harsh`

@export How much of my damages for loss of earning capacity will I get after the cap and, for a road accident, the further 20% discount (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 54, 56A(5))?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident; in another motor accident; in an accident caused by negligence or similar; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `the assessed damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages assessed for loss of earning capacity, past and future, in dollars: leaving out the first week you could not work (s 54(1)) and interest, and after the 5% discount rate and any reduction for contributory negligence
      `CPI Sep before` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the accident
      `CPI Sep 2001` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2001, on the same base
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much for loss of earning capacity after the cap and discount` MEANS
    `the damages for loss of earning capacity (ss 54(2), 56A(5))` `how it arose` `the accident day` `the assessed damages` `CPI Sep before` `CPI Sep 2001`

@export My family member died: what is the most we, the dependants, can get for economic loss from the death (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 54(3))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident that caused the death
      `damages already received` IS A NUMBER @desc Damages for loss of earning capacity the person received before they died, in dollars (0 if none)
      `CPI Sep before` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter of the year before the accident
      `CPI Sep 2001` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the September quarter 2001, on the same base
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most for dependants' economic loss` MEANS
    `the most for dependants' economic loss (s 54(3))` `the accident day` `damages already received` `CPI Sep before` `CPI Sep 2001`

@export After a road accident, what is the most I can get for lost superannuation contributions (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 56A(6)-(9))?
GIVEN `my earning capacity damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages payable to you for loss or impairment of the earning capacity the contributions are based on, in dollars
      `the minimum employer contribution rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum percentage of earnings the law requires to be paid as employer superannuation contributions (for the self-employed, the rate for an employee earning the same), for example 11.5
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most for lost superannuation` MEANS
    `the most for lost superannuation contributions (s 56A(6), (8))` `my earning capacity damages` `the minimum employer contribution rate`

@export What discount rate is used to turn my future losses into a lump sum (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 3(1), 55)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What discount rate applies to my future losses` MEANS `the prescribed discount rate (s 3(1), s 55)`

@export Can I get interest on this part of my damages, or damages for the cost of managing the award (Civil Liability Act 1936 ss 56, 57)?
GIVEN `the head` IS A `Head of damages` @desc The part of the damages: non-economic loss; future loss; past economic loss; or the cost of investing or managing the amount awarded
GIVETH A `Interest answer`
`Can I get interest on this part of my damages` MEANS
    `interest on this head (ss 56, 57)` `the head`

@export Can I get damages for the care my family gives me without pay (gratuitous services), and up to how much (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 58; Civil Liability Regulations 2013 reg 18)?
GIVEN `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident; in another motor accident; in an accident caused by negligence or similar; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `who` IS A `Who gives the services` @desc Who gives you the care: your parent, spouse, domestic partner or child, or someone else
      `the scale value` IS A NUMBER @desc The injury scale value (0 to 100) that applies to your injury (used only after an MVA motor accident)
      `hours a week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the care is (or will be) given
      `consecutive months` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months the care is (or will be) given
      `scheme needs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are (or were) a Lifetime Support Scheme participant and the care meets your assessed treatment, care and support needs from the injury
      `needed and would be paid otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied the care is reasonably required and someone would otherwise have to be paid to give it
      `SAWE` IS A NUMBER @desc State average weekly earnings: the latest ABS male full-time adult average weekly ordinary time earnings for South Australia, in dollars
GIVETH A `Gratuitous services answer`
`Can I get damages for my family's care` MEANS
    `damages for gratuitous services (s 58)` `how it arose` `the accident day` `who` `the scale value` `hours a week` `consecutive months` `scheme needs` `needed and would be paid otherwise` `SAWE`

@export After a road accident, what is the highest hourly rate allowed for the care my family gives me without pay (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 58(4)(b); Civil Liability Regulations 2013 reg 19)?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `CPI Mar adjustment` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the March quarter of the year of the last 1 July on or before the accident
      `CPI Mar 2013` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index (all groups, Adelaide) for the March quarter 2013, on the same base
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the highest hourly rate for family care` MEANS
    `the highest hourly rate for family care (s 58(4)(b), reg 19)` `the accident day` `CPI Mar adjustment` `CPI Mar 2013`

@export I am in the Lifetime Support Scheme: can I still get damages for my treatment, care and support needs (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 58A)?
GIVEN `a participant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are, or have been, a participant or interim participant in the Lifetime Support Scheme
      `needs from the injury during participation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the needs relate to the motor vehicle injury you are in the Scheme for and arise while you are a participant
GIVETH A `Scheme answer`
`Can a scheme participant get damages for care needs` MEANS
    `the Lifetime Support Scheme bar (s 58A)` `a participant` `needs from the injury during participation`

@export My family member died in a road accident: how much of our damages for loss of financial support is left after the further 20% discount (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 58B(3))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the road accident (an MVA motor accident) that caused the death
      `the assessed damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages assessed for loss of financial support, after the discount rate and any reduction for contributory negligence, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much for loss of financial support after the discount` MEANS
    `the damages for loss of financial support (s 58B(3))` `the accident day` `the assessed damages`

§§ `Other claims (Part 9)`

@export My spouse or domestic partner was injured: can I claim for the loss or impairment of our relationship (consortium) (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 65)?
GIVEN `I am the spouse or partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the injured person's spouse or domestic partner
      `wrongful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect or default
      `how it arose` IS A `How the injury arose` @desc How the injury arose: in an MVA motor accident; in another motor accident; in an accident caused by negligence or similar; or in some other way
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `the scale value` IS A NUMBER @desc The injury scale value (0 to 100) that applies to their injury (used only after an MVA motor accident)
GIVETH A `Consortium answer`
`Can I claim for loss of consortium` MEANS
    `loss of consortium (s 65)` `I am the spouse or partner` `wrongful` `how it arose` `the accident day` `the scale value`

@export Can I get damages for the costs of raising a child born after a failed sterilisation, contraceptive or abortion (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 67)?
GIVEN `the action` IS A `Kind of action about a child` @desc The kind of action: negligence causing an unintended conception; negligence causing a failed abortion; negligence causing a birth from a pregnancy that would have been aborted; innocent misrepresentation causing either; breach of warranty for a failed contraceptive; or another kind
      `the child is disabled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is mentally or physically disabled
GIVETH A `Raising a child answer`
`Can I get damages for raising the child` MEANS
    `the costs of raising a child (s 67)` `the action` `the child is disabled`

@export As personal representative of someone who died before their injury claim was resolved, can I claim damages for unreasonable delay against the other side (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 70)?
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `they had a right to damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their personal injury gave them a right to compensation or damages
      `they made a written claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they made a claim in writing, giving a reasonable indication of its grounds
      `unreasonable delay knowing the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone unreasonably delayed resolving the claim, knowing or when they ought to have known that age, illness or injury put the person at risk of dying first
      `the one claimed against or in control of the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that someone was the person the claim lay against, or controlled or had an interest in its defence
      `died before non-economic loss was settled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died before compensation or damages for non-economic loss were finally determined by agreement or judgment
      `no survival damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether no damages have been or can be recovered under s 3(2) of the Survival of Causes of Action Act 1940 (dust disease)
      `brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have brought this claim before the court or tribunal
      `the day brought` IS A DATE @desc The day you brought it (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Delay damages answer`
`Can I claim damages for unreasonable delay` MEANS
    `damages for unreasonable delay (s 70)` `the day of death` `they had a right to damages` `they made a written claim` `unreasonable delay knowing the risk` `the one claimed against or in control of the defence` `died before non-economic loss was settled` `no survival damages` `brought` `the day brought` `today`

@export By when must I bring a claim for damages for unreasonable delay in resolving a deceased person's injury claim (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 70(4))?
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the delay claim be brought` MEANS
    `the last day to bring the section 70 claim (s 70(4))` `the day of death`

@export Can I sue the person who came to help me in an emergency (a good samaritan) for what they did (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 74)?
GIVEN `unpaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they acted without expecting payment or other consideration
      `I needed emergency help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were apparently in need of emergency assistance (emergency medical help, or help because your life or safety was endangered)
      `phone advice from a medically qualified person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is about advice a medically qualified person (doctor, statutorily recognised health professional, ambulance officer or paramedic) gave about your treatment by telephone or other telecommunication
      `in good faith and not reckless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did was done in good faith and without recklessness
      `CTP covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability falls within compulsory third party motor vehicle insurance
      `they were impaired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their capacity to exercise due care and skill was significantly impaired by alcohol or another recreational drug at the time
GIVETH A `Good samaritan answer`
`Can I sue the good samaritan` MEANS
    `suing a good samaritan (s 74)` `unpaid` `I needed emergency help` `phone advice from a medically qualified person` `in good faith and not reckless` `CTP covers it` `they were impaired`

@export The person I am suing apologised: does the apology count as an admission that they were at fault (Civil Liability Act 1936 s 75)?
GIVEN `defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for defamation
GIVETH A `Apology answer`
`Does their apology count as an admission` MEANS
    `the effect of an apology (s 75)` `defamation`
